   "source": [
    "# Scatter plot with linear regression line\n",
    "plot3 = (\n",
    "    ggplot(ratings_complete, aes(x='tomatometer_rating', y='audience_rating')) +\n",
    "    geom_point() +\n",
    "    geom_smooth(method='lm', formula='y ~ x', se=False) +\n",
    "    labs(title='Scatter plot with Linear Regression', x='Tomatometer Rating', y='Audience Rating')\n",
    ")\n",
    "plot3"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "b40920c8-0260-4009-bbbf-55acdd34c165",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The black line represents the fitted regression line:\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\hat{y}_i = 34.5089 + 0.4461 x_i$\n",
    "\n",
    "or\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\widehat{audience\\_rating}_i = 34.5089 + 0.4461 * tomatometer\\_rating_i$ "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "3f5044e8-5de7-4535-801d-1dbae25dd756",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Interpretations:**\n",
    "\n",
    "Slope: For every unit increase in [x-variable], we expect [y-variable] to increase by $\\hat{\\beta}_1$ [y-units] on average.\n",
    "\n",
    "Ex: For every additional increase in the Tomatometer rating, we expect the audience rating to increase by 0.4461 points on average. \n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "Intercept: For an observation where [x-variable] is zero, we expect its [y-variable] to be $\\hat{\\beta}_0$ on average.\n",
    "\n",
    "(Example: https://www.forbes.com/sites/paultassi/2024/08/10/the-borderlands-movie-debuts-with-a-0-on-rotten-tomatoes/ ) \n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "Y-hat: For an observation where [x-variable] is x_i, we predict its [y-variable] to be $\\hat{\\beta}_0 + \\hat{\\beta}_1 x_i$.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "(Watch out for nonsense interpretations.) \n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "0cfaaadc-1b4a-427e-9150-8819787e1da7",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## How do I get that line???"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "ad2161ae-fe30-47b4-98db-bb46122849d8",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Linear regression in Python \n",
    "\n",
    "You can use `Scikit-learn` or `statsmodels` to do linear regression\n",
    "- `Scikit-learn` focus more on predictions, not much on inference\n",
    "- `statsmodels` is closer to the functions defined in R, has more inference\n",
    "- We are going to introduce `sklearn.linear_model`, `statsmodels.api.OLS` and `statsmodels.formula.api.ols` (most common). You are not limited to what I have introduced in class.  \n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "e5479f8c-7c62-4142-83de-6ca464d7d66b",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### 1. Scikit-learn"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 12,
   "id": "293eb5dd-de9a-4145-af8c-e6c7dc593ea1",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 13,
   "id": "4859e039-22e3-40ce-ae24-2b04913024af",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Fit linear model\n",
    "X = ratings_complete[['tomatometer_rating']]\n",
    "y = ratings_complete[['audience_rating']]\n",
    "model = LinearRegression().fit(X, y)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 14,
   "id": "4f893a9f-af7a-4c33-8ea9-604346fd420b",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "(array([34.50894913]), array([[0.4460597]]))"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 14,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "model.intercept_, model.coef_"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 15,
   "id": "70155084-e849-4579-a769-d64fd2364096",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Slope: [0.4460597], Intercept: [34.50894913]\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# Linear model summary\n",
    "slope = model.coef_[0]\n",
    "intercept = model.intercept_\n",
    "print(f\"Slope: {slope}, Intercept: {intercept}\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "3f301ca4-6a4c-4604-b755-80ff5d78cdfc",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "This corresponds to the fitted regression line:\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\hat{y}_i = 34.5089 + 0.4461 x_i$\n",
    "\n",
    "or\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\widehat{audience\\_rating}_i = 34.5089 + 0.4461 * tomatometer\\_rating_i$ \n"
   ]
  },
